## text
PLAN: 19 change(s), 4 suppressed  —  gate verdict BLOCK

  create-sequence    sales.order_seq
  alter-sequence     sales.invoice_seq
  create-enum-type   sales.colour
  add-enum-value     sales.status 'draft' (first)
  add-enum-value     sales.status 'shipped' after 'paid'
  add-enum-value     sales.single 'only' (first)
  create-domain-type sales.email
  create-domain-type sales.work_email
  drop-domain-not-null sales.code
  set-domain-default sales.code 'd'::text (was 'c'::text)
  drop-domain-constraint sales.code code_stale
  drop-domain-constraint sales.code code_len
  add-domain-constraint sales.code code_len CHECK ((length(VALUE) > 2))
  validate-domain-constraint sales.code code_validated
  set-domain-not-null sales.renamed_default
  drop-domain-default sales.renamed_default
  drop-sequence      sales.stale_seq
  drop-enum-type     sales.legacy
  drop-domain-type   sales.legacy_domain

NOT PROPOSED — differences Strata saw and will not act on:

  sales.ext_seq                [extension-owned]
      an extension owns this sequence
  sales.priority               [not-compared]
      the project orders this type's values differently from the database (project: low, high; database: high, low). PostgreSQL cannot reorder an enum's values, so nothing here can converge without recreating the type and everything declared with it.
  sales.unrendered             [not-compared]
      this domain's declaration could not be rendered by the server, so its base type, default and check constraints were not compared. A CREATE DOMAIN carries no predicate text through the parser; only the server can say what it means.
  sales.rebased                [not-compared]
      the project declares this domain over character varying(10) and the database has it over text; the project declares collation C and the database has the base type's. PostgreSQL has no ALTER DOMAIN ... TYPE and cannot change a domain's collation either, so this cannot converge without recreating the domain and every column declared with it. Nothing else about this domain was compared: its default and its check predicates are rendered through the base type, so while the base types differ the two sides can never agree on them.

  [ALLOW] creates sequence sales.order_seq
         why:  Additive. Nothing can already draw from a sequence that does not exist.
         next: Proceed.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] changes the increment, bounds, cache or cycle of sales.invoice_seq
         why:  The numbers it hands out change and nothing errors. Strata cannot list what draws from it: a sequence is named inside a column default, which the catalog reports as text rather than as a dependency.
         next: Confirm nothing depends on the current increment or bounds, then approve explicitly.

  [ALLOW] creates enum type sales.colour
         why:  Additive. Nothing can already be declared with a type that does not exist.
         next: Proceed.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] adds value 'draft' to enum type sales.status
         why:  Additive to the type, and two things follow that Strata cannot check. A value added inside a transaction cannot be USED in that same transaction, so a plan that also writes a row with it fails as a unit. And every exhaustive CASE over this type in application code is now missing an arm, which no schema check can see.
         next: Confirm nothing in this plan writes the new value, and that the code reading this type handles it.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] adds value 'shipped' to enum type sales.status
         why:  Additive to the type, and two things follow that Strata cannot check. A value added inside a transaction cannot be USED in that same transaction, so a plan that also writes a row with it fails as a unit. And every exhaustive CASE over this type in application code is now missing an arm, which no schema check can see.
         next: Confirm nothing in this plan writes the new value, and that the code reading this type handles it.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] adds value 'only' to enum type sales.single
         why:  Additive to the type, and two things follow that Strata cannot check. A value added inside a transaction cannot be USED in that same transaction, so a plan that also writes a row with it fails as a unit. And every exhaustive CASE over this type in application code is now missing an arm, which no schema check can see.
         next: Confirm nothing in this plan writes the new value, and that the code reading this type handles it.

  [ALLOW] creates domain sales.email
         why:  Additive. Nothing can already be declared with a type that does not exist.
         next: Proceed.

  [ALLOW] creates domain sales.work_email
         why:  Additive. Nothing can already be declared with a type that does not exist.
         next: Proceed.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] lets sales.code admit NULL again
         why:  Nothing breaks and nothing errors. What is lost is the promise that NULL cannot arrive in any column of this type — which code reading those columns may already rely on.
         next: Confirm the readers of columns of this type handle NULL, then approve explicitly.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] sets the default of sales.code to 'd'::text, replacing 'c'::text
         why:  Every insert that omits a column of this type stores a different value from now on, and nothing errors. Strata cannot list those columns: a column's type is rendered text in the catalog, not a dependency it models.
         next: Confirm the writes that rely on the current default, then approve explicitly.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] removes constraint code_stale from sales.code
         why:  Nothing breaks and nothing errors. What is lost is the promise that the excluded values cannot arrive in any column of this type.
         next: Confirm nothing relies on this constraint holding, then approve explicitly.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] removes constraint code_len from sales.code
         why:  Nothing breaks and nothing errors. What is lost is the promise that the excluded values cannot arrive in any column of this type.
         next: Confirm nothing relies on this constraint holding, then approve explicitly.

  [ALLOW] adds constraint code_len to sales.code: CHECK ((length(VALUE) > 2))
         why:  Tightening, and loud: PostgreSQL scans every column declared with this domain and the statement fails if a stored value would violate it. A plan is one transaction, so a failure costs the run rather than the data.
         next: Proceed.

  [ALLOW] validates constraint code_validated on sales.code
         why:  Checks the values this constraint was added NOT VALID and allowed to skip. It changes no data and removes nothing; it either passes or fails loudly, which is the point of running it.
         next: Proceed.

  [ALLOW] makes sales.renamed_default reject NULL
         why:  Tightening, and loud: PostgreSQL scans every column declared with this domain and the statement fails if one holds a NULL. A plan is one transaction, so a failure costs the run rather than the data.
         next: Proceed.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] removes the default from sales.renamed_default
         why:  Every insert that omitted a column of this type used to get a value and now gets NULL — or fails, where the column is NOT NULL. Strata cannot list those columns.
         next: Confirm no write relies on this default, then approve explicitly.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] drops sequence sales.stale_seq
         why:  A column default naming it keeps its text, so every insert relying on that default starts failing. Strata cannot list which defaults name it, for the same reason it cannot list a sequence's readers.
         next: Confirm no column default draws from it, then approve explicitly.

  [BLOCK] drops enum type sales.legacy
         why:  Dropping a type drops every column declared with it, and the data in those columns goes with them. PostgreSQL will refuse while a dependency exists, so this either fails or destroys something.
         next: If the type really is unused, drop the columns that use it first, in their own reviewed change.

  [BLOCK] drops domain sales.legacy_domain
         why:  Dropping a domain drops every column declared with it, and the data in those columns goes with them. PostgreSQL will refuse while a dependency exists, so this either fails or destroys something.
         next: If the domain really is unused, drop the columns that use it first, in their own reviewed change.


